(7669290, 1251576, 255536, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Ammie:</b></div><hr/><i> Congratulations on your first ribbon! and PB. Lovely entry </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thank you very much. Congratulations on your wonderful photo. I have been looking at your previous entries. You have some great shots.</td>', 'Originally posted by Ammie: Congratulations on your first ribbon! and PB. Lovely entry   Thank you very much. Congratulations on your wonderful photo. I have been looking at your previous entries. You have some great shots.', '2020-07-10 08:26:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(7669322, 1251122, 124815, '<td valign="top">My highest vote (8) - it kept me interested in what people alone are doing these days, at different speed and interest</td>', 'My highest vote (8) - it kept me interested in what people alone are doing these days, at different speed and interest', '2020-07-10 15:55:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(7669326, 1251593, 104230, '<td valign="top">I just noticed that the shutter speed was 1/2500!<br/> <br/>My 2 cents. A few years ago when I was playing around with panning I found that it can be hard to achieve a shallow depth of field and a long enough exposure time to capture noticeable panning unless you add a neutral density filter. Then again, when you are panning depth of field may not be much of an issue since much of the image will be fairly blurred.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2020-07-10 17:41:12.</i></td>', 'I just noticed that the shutter speed was 1/2500! My 2 cents. A few years ago when I was playing around with panning I found that it can be hard to achieve a shallow depth of field and a long enough exposure time to capture noticeable panning unless you add a neutral density filter. Then again, when you are panning depth of field may not be much of an issue since much of the image will be fairly blurred.', '2020-07-10 16:43:42.000000', '2020-07-10 18:41:12.000000', 0),
